CAT = "mypcePrinciples"; SEG = "PRN"
Q = [
("Under the Financial Services Act 2013, a consumer applying for a policy must:",
 "Take care not to misrepresent", ["Volunteer every fact about their life", "Pay a deposit before answering", "Have the proposal checked by a lawyer"],
 "Schedule 9 of the FSA replaced the old duty to volunteer facts: a consumer must take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation when answering the insurer's questions."),
("A company buying fire insurance for its factory has a duty to:",
 "Disclose matters relevant to the risk", ["Answer only the insurer's questions", "Disclose nothing beyond its address", "Wait for the insurer to inspect first"],
 "A non-consumer contract keeps a duty of disclosure: the business must disclose matters it knows, or a reasonable person would know, to be relevant to the insurer's decision."),
("A material fact is one that would:",
 "Sway a prudent insurer's decision", ["Interest the proposer's whole family", "Appear on the printed policy schedule", "Reduce the agent's commission"],
 "A fact is material if it would affect a prudent underwriter's decision to accept the risk or on what terms and premium."),
("Who owes the duty of utmost good faith in an insurance contract?",
 "Both insurer and insured", ["Only the person buying cover", "Only the insurer and its agent", "Only the beneficiary"],
 "Utmost good faith binds both parties. The insurer, for example, must not hide terms or exclusions that the proposer needs to understand."),
("A consumer deliberately hides a heart condition when answering the proposal form. The insurer may usually:",
 "Avoid it and refuse claims", ["Only charge a higher premium", "Pay the claim in full anyway", "Cancel it and refund double premium"],
 "Under Schedule 9 of the FSA, a deliberate or reckless misrepresentation lets the insurer avoid the contract and refuse all claims."),
("Which statement best describes the principle of indemnity?",
 "The insured is put back, not ahead", ["The insured gains from every claim", "The insured is paid the sum insured", "The insurer pays twice the loss"],
 "Indemnity restores the insured to the same financial position as just before the loss, without profit."),
("Why is a life policy not a contract of indemnity?",
 "A life has no exact money value", ["Life claims are never paid in full", "Life policies have no sum insured", "Life insurers cannot be sued"],
 "A life cannot be valued precisely, so the insurer promises an agreed sum. Life and personal accident death benefits are benefit contracts."),
("A motorist's insurer pays for his car repairs, then sues the driver who caused the crash. This is:",
 "Subrogation", ["Contribution", "Proximate cause", "Average"],
 "After paying a claim, the insurer takes over the insured's rights against the party at fault, so the insured cannot recover twice."),
("Subrogation does not apply to which kind of policy?",
 "Life insurance", ["Motor insurance", "Fire insurance", "Goods in transit insurance"],
 "Subrogation follows indemnity. Life insurance is not a contract of indemnity, so the life insurer has no right to sue a wrongdoer for the death."),
("A house is insured with Insurer X for RM 150,000 and Insurer Y for RM 50,000. A fire loss is RM 12,000. Under contribution, X pays:",
 "RM 9,000", ["RM 3,000", "RM 6,000", "RM 12,000"],
 "Rateable proportion: 150,000 of 200,000 total cover is three quarters, and three quarters of RM 12,000 is RM 9,000. Y pays the other RM 3,000."),
("When does the principle of contribution apply?",
 "Two policies cover one loss", ["The insured caused the loss", "A third party caused the loss", "The policy has a waiting period"],
 "Contribution shares a loss between insurers when more than one indemnity policy covers the same interest, so the insured is not paid twice."),
("A fire, which is covered, leads firefighters to soak the ground floor while putting it out. The water damage is treated as caused by:",
 "The fire", ["The firefighters", "The water supply", "An excluded flood"],
 "Damage done in fighting an insured fire flows directly from the fire, which is the proximate cause, so it is covered as fire damage."),
("A storm, which is covered, blows down a wall, and rain then soaks the stock inside. The proximate cause of the water damage is:",
 "The storm", ["The rain", "The wall", "Poor storage"],
 "The storm set off an unbroken chain of events ending in the water damage, so it is the proximate cause."),
("A buyer of a life policy on her own life has an insurable interest because:",
 "Everyone may insure their own life", ["She signs the proposal form herself", "She pays the first premium in cash", "Her agent confirms it in writing"],
 "A person always has an insurable interest in their own life. The FSA also recognises interest in a spouse, a child under the age of majority and a dependant."),
("A man sells his car on Monday and it is stolen on Tuesday. He cannot claim under his old policy because he no longer had:",
 "An insurable interest", ["A no-claim discount", "A named driver", "An excess to pay"],
 "For property, insurable interest must exist at the time of the loss. Once the car was sold, the seller suffered no loss when it was stolen."),
("Which person has an insurable interest in a house under a housing loan?",
 "The bank holding the charge", ["The owner's next-door neighbour", "The developer's contractor", "The previous tenant of the house"],
 "A chargee bank has a financial interest because the house secures its loan, so it can insure the property or be named on the owner's policy."),
("A shop worth RM 400,000 is insured for RM 300,000 under a policy with average. A loss is RM 40,000. The claim paid is:",
 "RM 30,000", ["RM 10,000", "RM 32,000", "RM 40,000"],
 "Average: 300,000 / 400,000 x 40,000 = RM 30,000. The owner bears the rest for under-insuring."),
("An insurer pays to rebuild a damaged wall rather than pay cash. This method of indemnity is:",
 "Reinstatement", ["Subrogation", "Contribution", "Salvage recovery"],
 "Indemnity can be given by cash, repair, replacement or reinstatement, which restores the property to its former condition."),
("An excess in a policy means the insured:",
 "Pays the first part of each claim", ["Is paid more than the actual loss", "Pays no premium in the first year", "Can claim from two insurers"],
 "An excess, or deductible, is the amount of each loss the insured pays before the insurer pays the rest."),
("After a total loss claim, the damaged car belongs to the insurer. This value is called:",
 "Salvage", ["Betterment", "Surrender value", "Excess"],
 "When the insurer pays a total loss it takes the remains (salvage), otherwise the insured would profit by keeping them."),
]
